== sports.f01 | feature | three-act
title: Lane Eight
genre: sports + drama
tone: Chlorine, early mornings and stubborn tenderness
era: - | contemporary
setting: Tolka Road Municipal Baths, Dublin | fictional | institution
logline: A sixty-one-year-old lifeguard who never learned to race enters the national masters' 1500 metres to stop the council closing her pool, coached by the fourteen-year-old she once pulled out of it.
premise: {maeve} has guarded the Tolka Road pool for thirty-four years and swum a mile in its outside lane every morning before opening. The council will close it in March unless it can show "competitive use". Masters' swimming counts. {maeve} has never dived off a block. {kofi}, a club swimmer banned from his own squad for fighting, needs forty hours of community service and knows what a tumble turn is.
synopsis: The council's letter gives the baths until the spring estimates. {imelda}, the manager, finds the loophole: a pool that hosts registered competitive swimmers is a sports facility, not a leisure one, and is funded from a different budget. She needs one name on one national entry list. {maeve} refuses, then reads the qualifying time and realises that she has been swimming inside it, untimed, for a decade. The first act is a woman discovering that something she did for quiet is measurable. {kofi} arrives sullen, serving his hours by mopping, and cannot watch her turns without wincing. He starts correcting her from the deck. He is a brutal coach, as his own was to him, and {maeve}, who has spent her life watching water for the moment a child goes under, sees precisely what is wrong with him. In the second act they train: blocks she is afraid of, a racing dive that empties her lungs, turns that cost her four seconds each until they don't. {kofi}'s former coach {declan} wants him back for a relay and wants him away from "the old one". {maeve}'s brother {fintan}, a councillor, votes for the closure in committee and comes to tell her himself, and she finds she is angrier at being pitied than at being closed. At the regional qualifier she false-starts, is allowed one more, and swims the time by two seconds. The council notes the entry and schedules the vote for the Monday after nationals: one swimmer is not use; a result might be. The third act is the race. {kofi} is offered his place back on the same weekend and takes it, then walks off the relay deck when {declan} screams at a ten-year-old. He reaches the masters' pool as her heat is called. She is in lane eight, the outside lane, the one she has swum in all her life. She does not win; she comes fifth of eight and takes forty seconds off her time. What matters is the last two hundred metres, when the swimmers in lanes six and seven fade and she doesn't, because nobody ever taught her to go out too fast. On the Monday the vote is seven to six to keep the baths for a year, {fintan} changing sides. {kofi} starts a Saturday squad for children nobody else will coach. {maeve} is on the deck with a whistle, watching the water.
question: Can something done for love survive being counted?
themes: late competence; what coaching is for; public places as inheritance
audience: Families and adults; anyone who has kept a routine alive
viewpoint: Shared between {maeve} and {kofi}; underwater we are always with her
stakes: The baths and eleven jobs; {kofi}'s last chance not to become his coach; {maeve}'s quiet, which she has to spend to save the place that gave it to her
conflict: A woman who swims to be left alone against a system that only values what it can time, and a boy who knows only one way to make someone faster
ending: Fifth of eight, a personal best, and a pool kept for a year by one vote. {maeve} goes back to guarding. {kofi} coaches the Saturday children, quietly.
experience: The pleasure of watching someone get better at something, a race you feel in your lungs, and a small civic victory that feels enormous
look: Green-white tile, steamed windows, winter dark outside at six in the morning; underwater shots long and silent; the nationals pool too bright and too blue
sound: Water from under and over, a pace clock, a whistle. Breathing as the score in the race.
m: keep | institution | self-vs-others | bittersweet
@ maeve | Maeve Gallagher | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 61 | To keep the baths open and be left in peace | It is the only place she has ever been good at being | To save it she has to be watched, timed and beaten | Fearless in water and shy on a starting block | From swimming to be alone to swimming in front of people | Sparing, dry, Dublin; whistles instead of shouting | Red lifeguard polo, towelling robe, goggle marks that never fade
@ kofi | Kofi Lynch-Boateng | co-lead | deuteragonist | human | he/him | 14 | To get back on his squad | Swimming is the one thing he was told he was for | He coaches the way he was coached, and it is breaking him | Gifted, furious, kind when he forgets himself | From shouting splits to counting her lengths under his breath | Sarcastic, technical, voice not finished breaking | Club hoodie with the crest picked off, stopwatch on a shoelace
@ imelda | Imelda Rooney | supporting | ally | human | she/her | 47 | A budget line that keeps eleven people employed | She took the job to save the place and has become its undertaker | The loophole needs a result, not just an entry | Bureaucratic cunning in the service of sentiment | From using {maeve} to standing in the gallery shouting | Fast, apologetic, speaks in council phrases she hates | Lanyard, fleece, a folder that is always open
@ declan | Declan Furlong | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 50 | Medals, and {kofi} back to win them | He was a nearly-man himself | His method works, on the ones it doesn't finish | Dedicated, magnetic, cruel on the clock | Unchanged; he is what {kofi} could become | Loud across water, intimate up close | Club tracksuit, clipboard, a whistle he never needs
@ fintan | Councillor Fintan Gallagher | supporting | foil | human | he/him | 64 | A balanced estimate and his sister's forgiveness | He learned to swim in that pool and left it behind | He has already voted once to close it | Reasonable to a fault | From pitying her to voting with her | Smooth, explanatory, calls her "Mae" when losing | Good coat, council pin, wet shoes he didn't plan for
@ sorcha | Sorcha Devine | featured | none | human | she/her | 10 | To not be shouted at | She is the smallest on {declan}'s squad | She is the child {kofi} was | Brave in the water, silent out of it | From flinching to turning up on a Saturday | Whispers; counts strokes aloud | Squad cap too big for her
~ maeve - kofi | lifeguard and the boy she once pulled out | he mops; she ignores him | he counts her lengths; she watches his children
~ kofi - declan | swimmer and coach | exile and a conditional way back | {kofi} walks off the deck
~ maeve - fintan | sister and brother | he comes to explain the vote | he changes it
~ maeve - imelda | lifeguard and manager | a favour asked badly | two women in a gallery
~ kofi - sorcha | older and younger squad-mates | he doesn't notice her | she is first through the door on Saturday
# The Letter | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Pool Hall | DAWN | maeve | Show the mile | Six o'clock, lights half on. {maeve} swims her mile in the outside lane, alone, unhurried, and gets out as the first regulars arrive | | 4 | water, props=pace clock+whistle
# The Letter | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Office | DAY | maeve, imelda | Ask the favour | {imelda} reads the council's letter, then the rulebook, then a qualifying time. {maeve} says no and looks at the number twice | {maeve} has been inside the time for years | 4 | props=council letter+entry form
# The Letter | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Pool Hall | DAY | maeve, kofi | Meet the coach | {kofi}, mopping, watches her open turn and says, to nobody, that it is costing her four seconds a length. She asks him to show her | A fourteen-year-old takes charge | 5 | water, props=mop+stopwatch
# The Work | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Pool Hall | NIGHT | maeve, kofi | Fail at the block | After closing. She stands on a starting block for the first time, cannot make herself fall, and climbs down. He shouts. She tells him, level, exactly what shouting does to a person in water | {kofi} hears his coach in his own mouth | 6 | water, stunts=racing dive
# The Work | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Viewing Gallery | DAY | maeve, fintan | Be pitied | {fintan} comes to tell her he voted for closure before she hears it elsewhere, and that it isn't personal. She finds she minds the kindness most | | 4
# The Work | INT | Regional Pool, Starting End | DAY | maeve, kofi, imelda | Qualify | A false start, one more chance, thirty lengths. {kofi} walks the deck beside lane one counting for her on his fingers. Two seconds inside | {maeve} is entered for the nationals | 6 | water, crowd=60, stunts=racing dive, props=lap counter
# The Race | INT | National Aquatic Centre, Relay Deck | DAY | kofi, declan, sorcha | Choose | {kofi}, reinstated, on the blocks for the relay. {declan} tears into {sorcha} for a slow changeover. {kofi} takes his cap off and walks | {kofi} leaves the squad for good | 4 | water, crowd=200
# The Race | INT | National Aquatic Centre, Competition Pool | DAY | maeve, kofi, imelda, fintan | Swim it | Lane eight. She goes out last and stays there for a kilometre. With two hundred to go lanes six and seven come back to her, and she passes them without changing her stroke. Fifth. Forty seconds off | The result the council asked for | 9 | water, crowd=300, stunts=racing dive, props=lap counter
# The Race | INT | Tolka Road Baths, Pool Hall | DAY | maeve, kofi, sorcha, imelda | Keep it | A Saturday. Six children in mismatched caps, {sorcha} first in. {kofi} coaches without raising his voice. {maeve} sits in the high chair with her whistle, watching the water | | 3 | water, crowd=12, props=whistle+kickboards
